            <ArticleTitle>Is obesity a prognostic factor for acute myeloid leukemia outcome?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>359-65</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00277-011-1319-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Obesity adversely affects outcome in pediatric acute lymphocytic leukemia and acute myeloid leukemia (AML). We asked if obesity, measured by body mass index (BMI), affected outcome in 329 adult AML patients treated with high-dose cytarabine and idarubicin-containing regimens administered according to actual body weight. Age ≥ 60, unfavorable karyotype, secondary AML, and positive smoking status had adverse impact on overall survival in a multivariate analysis, while BMI did not. We conclude that high BMI should not be a barrier to administer high-dose cytarabine-containing regimens for AML induction.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
